            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"NOVEL\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus identifies 'living creatures' symbolically as heavenly throne guardians (cherubim), the four Evangelists, the totality of animate creation, or attributes of Christ, based almost entirely on the visions in Ezekiel 1/10 and Revelation 4/5.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines a 'living creature' as a nation or organized people, treating it as the living counterpart to the prophetic symbol of a beast.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Angelic throne attendants (cherubim), the four Evangelists, or representative animate creation.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A nation or organized people.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"NO_CONSENSUS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"NO_BASELINE\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus applies the term to heavenly beings or cosmic representatives in throne room scenes, whereas the book applies it to human political entities in earthly judgment scenes.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book completely bypasses the heavenly attendant/Evangelist associations to redefine 'living creature' as a political or national entity, drawing on 'beast' symbolism instead of 'cherubim' symbolism.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"None of the consensus responses list 'nation or organized people' as a recognized interpretation for 'living creature'. The book introduces a totally novel meaning by structurally equating 'living creature' with apocalyptic 'beasts' (such as those in Daniel 7, which explicitly represent kingdoms).\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The biblical texts carefully distinguish between heavenly 'living creatures' (Hebrew 'chayyah', Greek 'zōon') in Ezekiel and Revelation 4, and the monstrous 'beasts' (Aramaic 'cheyva', Greek 'thērion') of Daniel 7 and Revelation 13. Conflating the two to mean 'nations' overlooks the distinct vocabulary and theological function of heavenly throne guardians.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
